From: xcentaurix
Subject: [PATCH] WPE: join and register with Nexus before any EGL call, on Nexus-based Broadcom boxes

On a Nexus-based Broadcom box (GigaBlue gbquad4kpro confirmed 2026-09-23; likely other Nexus-middleware boxes too, e.g. VU+ Ultimo 4K - same "Memory
interface not registered" signature, not yet retested with this exact fix), eglInitialize() aborts:

  ASSERT at libs/platform/bcg_abstract/gmem_abstract.c:132:gmem_init
  Memory interface not registered

Root-caused with a standalone diagnostic tool (tools/vuplus-probe in the hbbtv2 repo; see vu_egl_probe.c and project memory
project_hbbtv2_wpe_orb_plan.md for the full trace through dynsym_dump symbol listings and Broadcom's own header dump) rather than guessed at:

 - NxClient_Join() (libnxclient.so) - the general Nexus client/server IPC join - is necessary but, confirmed by testing, NOT sufficient alone: it
   succeeds (returns 0) but eglInitialize() still asserts identically afterward.
 - NXPL_RegisterNexusDisplayPlatform() (libnxpl.so) is the missing piece. Broadcom's own header (hbbtv2/headers/gb-nexus-headers/default_nexus.h,
   "Default Nexus platform API for EGL driver") declares it and documents it as: "Register a display for exclusive use ... It will register its own
   memory, h/w and display APIs suitable for basic exclusive mode rendering on a Nexus display." - matching the assertion text exactly.
 - In multi-process client mode (the normal case here, not SINGLE_PROCESS) NEXUS_DISPLAYHANDLE is just void*, not a real handle from the
   single-process-only NEXUS_Display_Open() - passing NULL works and was verified end-to-end: eglInitialize() succeeds (EGL 1.5) immediately after
   this call, in an isolated test tool, with the live TV picture confirmed unaffected during the test.

v1 of this patch only hooked PlatformDisplayDefault::create() and, deployed to a real GigaBlue box, the WebProcess still crashed with the identical
assertion. Root cause: WebProcess::platformInitializeWebProcess() constructs a PlatformDisplayLibWPE directly whenever a WPE Platform API renderer
backend is in use (m_rendererBufferTransportMode empty) - which is exactly this fork's own situation (the wpe_fb embedder) - and does NOT go through
PlatformDisplayDefault/initializePlatformDisplayIfNeeded() at all in that case. PlatformDisplayLibWPE::create() calls eglGetDisplay()/
eglGetPlatformDisplayEXT() directly, bypassing the original fix entirely. The standalone vu_egl_probe.c test that verified the underlying
NxClient_Join+NXPL_RegisterNexusDisplayPlatform sequence never caught this, because it calls EGL directly too, with no libwpe involved - it proved
the fix works, not that it was wired into the code path WebKit actually uses.

Fix (v2): the join+register logic now lives in a small shared header (NexusEGLPlatformQuirk.h) and is called from BOTH PlatformDisplayLibWPE::create()
(the real path) and PlatformDisplayDefault::create() (the generic fallback, kept in case some other WPE embedder/configuration on this fork ever
takes that path), each unregistering in its own destructor. Pure runtime detection via dlopen/dlsym, no Nexus SDK headers and no link-time dependency
on libnxclient.so/libnxpl.so, so this is a true no-op - confirmed via a regression test on the DM900 (which has neither library) that produces zero
related log output through either PlatformDisplay class - on any platform without them.

v2 (r8), deployed to a real GigaBlue box, made real progress: the WebProcess now got as far as loading a real page and starting an ORB application
(RunApp/CreateApplication all succeeded) before the identical "Memory interface not registered" abort - and the log confirmed NxClient_Join()
actually ran and returned 0 this time. But the expected "NXPL_RegisterNexusDisplayPlatform() -> handle=..." line never appeared, with no error
either: the dlopen("libnxpl.so")/dlsym() step was failing silently, and v2 had no diagnostics for that failure mode at all.

Fix (v3): every failure branch (both libraries, both dlopen and dlsym) now logs via dlerror()/a named message, so the next capture says exactly
which step failed and why instead of going silent. Also added an explicit dlopen("libnexus.so", RTLD_NOW|RTLD_GLOBAL) before libnxpl.so: libnxpl.so's
own dynamic symbol table (confirmed via dynsym_dump, see project memory) imports many libnexus.so symbols directly (NEXUS_Graphicsv3d_*/
NEXUS_Surface_*/NEXUS_MemoryBlock_* etc.) that RTLD_NOW must resolve immediately, unlike libnxclient.so's smaller, more self-contained symbol set -
a missing libnexus.so at that point would explain libnxpl.so's dlopen failing where libnxclient.so's just-preceding one did not. Harmless either way
(a no-op refcount bump if libnexus.so is already loaded by something else). Regression-tested on the DM900 again: all three new dlopen attempts fail
cleanly and are logged (confirmed neither library exists there, exactly as expected), page still loads normally, no regression.

v3 (r9), deployed to a real GigaBlue box, gave the actual answer: "dlopen(\"libnxpl.so\") failed: /usr/lib/libnxpl.so: undefined symbol:
BEGL_PlatformAboutToShutdown". That symbol is exported by libv3ddriver.so (= libEGL.so/libGLESv2.so on this box), which libnxpl.so also imports -
but this build uses libepoxy (USE(LIBEPOXY) in PlatformDisplayLibWPE.cpp), which lazily dlopens the real vendor libEGL.so/libGLESv2.so on the FIRST
actual EGL call, not at process startup. ensureNexusPlatformJoined() runs BEFORE any EGL call by design, so libv3ddriver.so simply wasn't loaded yet
at that point - explaining why NxClient_Join (needing only libnxclient.so's own, self-contained symbols) succeeded while libnxpl.so's dlopen(RTLD_NOW),
needing BEGL_* symbols from a library nothing had loaded yet, failed. This is exactly what tools/vuplus-probe/vu_egl_probe.c already does (and which
worked end-to-end, verified 2026-09-23): its own very first step dlopens an EGL-providing library, before any Nexus join/register call, so
libv3ddriver.so is already loaded as a side effect by the time it reaches NXPL_RegisterNexusDisplayPlatform.

Fix (v4): explicitly preload an EGL-providing library (same fallback name list as the probe: libEGL.so.1, libEGL.so, libv3ddriver.so - some
Nexus-based Broadcom images have no libEGL.so at all) right before libnxpl.so. Regression-tested on the DM900 again: the preload succeeds there
(finds the DM900's own libEGL.so, harmless), libnxpl.so still fails cleanly afterward (doesn't exist on DM900), page loads normally, no regression.

---
diff --git a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/NexusEGLPlatformQuirk.h b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/NexusEGLPlatformQuirk.h
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..e11cc9da
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/NexusEGLPlatformQuirk.h
@@ -0,0 +1,145 @@
+/*
